@@ -0,0 +1,54 @@
+# The Daily Grind origin migration
+
+This static app now has an install manifest and a two-origin migration build. No
+production origins or existing installed-app identity were provided, so
+`migration.config.json` uses reserved example subdomains. Nothing is deployed by
+the build.
+
+1. Set `oldOrigin` and `newOrigin` to your HTTPS origins. They must have the same
+   registrable domain; the build checks the Public Suffix List, including private
+   domains. Keep `oldAppId` exactly equal to the existing installed app's manifest
+   ID (or its previous resolved start URL if it had no explicit ID). This starter
+   assumes `/`. Set the destination identity with `newAppId`.
+2. List the localStorage configuration keys in `settingsKeys`. This repository
+   previously had no configuration persistence; the default keys are
+   `daily-grind:preferences` and `daily-grind:settings`. Only these explicit keys
+   move. Do not add authentication tokens. Cookies, IndexedDB, permissions, and
+   server-side accounts require their own migration if later introduced.
+3. Run `pnpm install` and `pnpm build`. An alternate JSON configuration can be
+   supplied with `pnpm build ./production-migration.json`.
+4. Deploy `dist/new` to the destination first, then `dist/old` to the original
+   origin. Both folders are standalone static sites. Include the hidden
+   `.well-known` directory. Do not deploy the repository root or redirect the old
+   site: its manifest, association, page, and settings bridge must stay reachable.
+
+The old association explicitly authorizes the exact destination manifest ID. Its
+manifest advertises `migrate_to`; the destination declares `migrate_from` with
+suggested behavior. Supporting browsers handle the installation update, including
+OS integration, through their own consent UI. Browsers without this feature get a
+link to the new site and instructions to install there; shortcuts cannot be
+preserved automatically on those browsers. See the
+[Chrome origin migration documentation](https://developer.chrome.com/blog/seamless-pwa-origin-migration).
+If adapting an existing PWA, retain its name, icons, manifest URL, and identity on
+the old origin rather than replacing its manifest with a new app identity.
+
+On the first destination visit, a hidden old-origin frame reads the allowlisted
+settings. Both sides verify the exact origin and source window, and the response
+must match a random request nonce. Settings never appear in URLs. Destination
+values win conflicts; the old data is never deleted. Successful import is marked
+so later visits do not re-import stale source settings. A failed write rolls back
+new entries and leaves retry available. Download/import provides a fallback for
+blocked frame storage; it copies only configuration, not the installation.
+
+The generated `_headers` file is for hosts supporting that format (such as
+Netlify or Cloudflare Pages). Other hosts must configure equivalent HTTP headers:
+serve the association as JSON, the manifest as `application/manifest+json`, and
+avoid caching configuration and the bridge. Permit only the new origin to frame
+the old bridge using CSP `frame-ancestors`. Do not apply `X-Frame-Options: DENY`
+or `SAMEORIGIN` to that bridge; any destination CSP must allow `frame-src` for the
+old origin. HTTPS and continued old-origin availability are required. Offline
+caching is intentionally not added to this previously static site.
+
+`pnpm test` runs storage/security and build-output checks without a browser or
+server. Deployment and actual browser-managed shortcut migration must happen
+outside this local build.
